From d5a06d3dc53266bdcc3613462402542f64683d24 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Micheal Waltz <micheal@ecliptik.com>
Date: Mon, 18 May 2026 22:18:51 -0700
Subject: [PATCH] dos: Phase 11 patch 0063 -- WaitDevice ring-drain timeout +
 resume (486 quit-hang fix)

486-class hardware-coverage campaign Bug 2: doskutsu v1.0.0 hangs on
quit-to-DOS on the Intel 486DX2-50 and 486DX2-66 (all six M2/M3 cells;
the Am5x86-133 and the Pentium OverDrive quit cleanly). crashcap
captured no fault -- it is a hang, not a DJGPP crash. The hung SDL logs
stop dead at snd-shutdown[4/6] pre-MIX_DestroyMixer, with no "audio
close:" line -- so DOSSOUNDBLASTER_CloseDevice is never entered; the
hang is upstream of it, in the SDL3 audio-playback-thread teardown
join that MIX_DestroyMixer -> SDL_QuitSubSystem(SDL_INIT_AUDIO) runs.

Root cause: DOSSOUNDBLASTER_WaitDevice is an unbounded for(;;) spin.
Its only two exits are (1) the ring has space, or (2) device->shutdown
is set (the patch-0046 bail). When the DSP idle-pause has engaged
(isr_dsp_paused -> IRQ-5 stopped -> the ring never drains), exit (1)
is dead. Exit (2) depends on the engine winning device->lock during
MIX_DestroyMixer to set device->shutdown -- a cross-thread wait that,
on the slower-bus DX2 configs, wedges. The audio thread then never
leaves its SDL_PlaybackAudioThreadIterate body and the teardown join
never completes.

Structural finding: the DSP-resume request that would un-stick the
ring is originated ONLY in DOSSOUNDBLASTER_PlayDevice -- which runs
AFTER WaitDevice within one Iterate, so it is unreachable while
WaitDevice spins. WaitDevice calls MaybeServiceIdlePauseRequests()
every iteration but only ever SERVICES a request, never ORIGINATES
one. So a "ring full + DSP paused" state is unrecoverable.

Fix, both in DOSSOUNDBLASTER_WaitDevice, both under one killswitch:
- Part 1: when the loop sees the ring full AND isr_dsp_paused, set
  g_dos_audio_resume_request = true. The next iteration's
  MaybeServiceIdlePauseRequests() issues the 0xD6/0xD4 resume, IRQ-5
  restarts, the ring drains. This is the real fix -- quit completes
  cleanly with no stall. Gated !g_dos_audio_force_dsp_pause to mirror
  PlayDevice's resume gate.
- Part 2: a 1000 ms wall-clock backstop on the spin. If the ring
  still has not drained (IRQ-5 wedged by a HW-IO-timing variant that
  part 1 did not lift -- DOSBox-X never reproduced this per the
  dosbox-not-proxy rule), WaitDevice returns true anyway so the
  Iterate completes, the audio thread re-checks device->shutdown, and
  the join proceeds. Wall-clock timed (SDL_GetTicksNS), so it is
  CPU/bus-speed independent; the ~1000 ms vs ~23 ms IRQ-period margin
  (~43x) means it cannot false-trigger in normal play.

Both parts sit under one killswitch so the off path is a true revert:
correctness fix -> ships default-ON with a strict-"0" killswitch
SDL_HINT_DOSKUTSU_DOS_AUDIO_WAITDEVICE_TIMEOUT. With =0 NOTHING 0063
added runs -- WaitDevice is the verbatim pre-0063 unbounded spin --
so the operator's 486DX2 A/B run can reproduce the pre-fix hang
cleanly. OpenDevice resolves the hint once and emits a boot banner.
One TU touched (src/audio/dos/SDL_dosaudio_sb.c).

In-game behavior: WaitDevice is a hot-path function, but the 0063
additions are inert during normal play -- part 1 only fires on "ring
full AND DSP idle-paused" (a state that does not arise in normal
gameplay; the DSP is idle-paused only when the ring is drained, not
full), and part 2 cannot fire because the ring drains every ~23 ms
via IRQ-5, three orders of magnitude under the 1000 ms bound. The
only in-game effect is a few extra SDL_GetTicksNS reads during the
brief ring-full spin windows. The genuine behavior change is confined
to the quit/teardown path.

Real-HW verification is the operator's 486DX2 in the 486 follow-up
iter; DOSBox-X cannot reproduce the hang. Workspace-local per the
SDL AI policy; never upstreamed.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 4.7 <noreply@anthropic.com>
---
 src/audio/dos/SDL_dosaudio_sb.c | 113 ++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++
 1 file changed, 113 insertions(+)

diff --git a/src/audio/dos/SDL_dosaudio_sb.c b/src/audio/dos/SDL_dosaudio_sb.c
index 4dbc05f54..33a7e7bd7 100644
--- a/src/audio/dos/SDL_dosaudio_sb.c
+++ b/src/audio/dos/SDL_dosaudio_sb.c
@@ -153,6 +153,26 @@ static bool g_dos_silence_detect_enabled = true;
                        fps lever; predicted +3 to +5 fps vs PLAY1) */
 static bool g_dos_silence_shape_d_active = false;
 
+/* DOSKUTSU patch (Phase 11 patch 0063): killswitch state for the
+   DOSSOUNDBLASTER_WaitDevice ring-drain timeout backstop. Default ON
+   (false = backstop active). Set
+   SDL_HINT_DOSKUTSU_DOS_AUDIO_WAITDEVICE_TIMEOUT=0 to flip true and
+   disable -- WaitDevice then reverts to the pre-0063 unbounded ring-
+   drain spin. Per CLAUDE.md strict-"0" env-var convention: only the
+   literal "0" disables. Resolved once at OpenDevice time; read by
+   DOSSOUNDBLASTER_WaitDevice. Correctness fix -- ships default-ON with
+   this killswitch per [[iter_discipline]] (correctness with
+   killswitch). */
+static bool g_dos_audio_waitdevice_timeout_disabled = false;
+
+/* Wall-clock bound for the WaitDevice ring-drain spin. The IRQ-5 ISR
+   drains one chunk per ~23 ms at the configured rate; 1000 ms is a
+   ~43x margin, so a normal-play stall can never reach it. If the spin
+   does reach it, IRQ-5 is wedged (the DSP idle-pause did not lift) and
+   WaitDevice must return anyway so the audio-thread Iterate completes
+   and the quit-to-DOS teardown join can proceed. */
+#define DOS_AUDIO_WAITDEVICE_TIMEOUT_NS  SDL_MS_TO_NS(1000)
+
 /* DOSKUTSU patch (Phase 11 patch 0058): Shape A mechanism-histogram
    instrumentation state. Diagnostic backstop for wave-36 PLAY6 Shape
    D fps lever per `pressure_test_tidy_narratives.md` discipline: if
@@ -686,6 +706,13 @@ static bool DOSSOUNDBLASTER_WaitDevice(SDL_AudioDevice *device)
     const Uint64 _p12_wait_t0 =
         g_p12_sdl_audio_instr_active ? SDL_GetTicksNS() : 0;
 
+    /* DOSKUTSU patch (Phase 11 patch 0063): wall-clock anchor for the
+       ring-drain timeout backstop. 0 == not yet waiting; set on the
+       first full-ring iteration so the off-path (ring has room -- the
+       common case in steady-state playback) pays no SDL_GetTicksNS
+       read. */
+    Uint64 _wd_wait_start_ns = 0;
+
     for (;;) {
         /* DOSKUTSU patch (Phase 10 patch 0046): bail out early when the
            audio device is shutting down. SDL3 audio core sets
@@ -726,6 +753,70 @@ static bool DOSSOUNDBLASTER_WaitDevice(SDL_AudioDevice *device)
             }
             return true; // room for at least one chunk
         }
+
+        /* DOSKUTSU patch (Phase 11 patch 0063): the ring is full. Both
+           the part-1 DSP-resume kick and the part-2 wall-clock backstop
+           sit under one killswitch so SDL_HINT_DOSKUTSU_DOS_AUDIO_
+           WAITDEVICE_TIMEOUT=0 is a TRUE revert to the pre-0063
+           unbounded ring-drain spin -- nothing 0063 added runs in that
+           mode. That clean revert is what the operator's 486DX2 A/B
+           run needs to reproduce the pre-fix quit hang. Default
+           (killswitch unset) runs both parts. */
+        if (!g_dos_audio_waitdevice_timeout_disabled) {
+            /* Part 1 -- originate a DSP resume from WaitDevice. The
+               ring is full; the only thing that drains it is the IRQ-5
+               ISR. If the idle-pause has engaged (isr_dsp_paused) the
+               ISR is not running, so this wait can never end on its
+               own. The resume request is normally originated by
+               DOSSOUNDBLASTER_PlayDevice (line ~1628) -- but PlayDevice
+               runs AFTER WaitDevice within one
+               SDL_PlaybackAudioThreadIterate, so it is structurally
+               unreachable while we spin here. WaitDevice is the only
+               point that can break the wedge. Set the request; the
+               MaybeServiceIdlePauseRequests() at the top of the next
+               loop iteration issues the 0xD6/0xD4 resume, IRQ-5
+               restarts, and the ring drains. Gated on
+               !g_dos_audio_force_dsp_pause to mirror PlayDevice's
+               resume gate (do not auto-resume in the force-pause
+               fps-measurement mode). This is the real fix for
+               486-campaign Bug 2 (the quit-to-DOS hang): see
+               docs/internal/486-CLASS-CAMPAIGN-FINDINGS.md sec.9. */
+            if (isr_dsp_paused && !g_dos_audio_force_dsp_pause) {
+                g_dos_audio_resume_request = true;
+            }
+
+            /* Part 2 -- wall-clock backstop. If the ring still has not
+               drained after DOS_AUDIO_WAITDEVICE_TIMEOUT_NS, IRQ-5 is
+               wedged (part 1's resume did not lift it -- a HW-IO-timing
+               variant the 486-class campaign surfaced on the Intel DX2
+               configs, where DOSBox-X never reproduced it per
+               [[dosbox_not_proxy]]). An unbounded spin here holds the
+               audio thread inside its SDL_PlaybackAudioThreadIterate
+               body; SDL/0046's device->shutdown bail cannot fire
+               because the engine sets device->shutdown only after
+               winning device->lock during the MIX_DestroyMixer
+               teardown -- so quit-to-DOS hangs hard. Returning true
+               ends the spin: the Iterate completes, the audio thread
+               re-checks device->shutdown on its next loop, and the
+               teardown join proceeds. Wall-clock timed (SDL_GetTicksNS),
+               so it is CPU/bus-speed independent -- an iteration count
+               would scale with the slower DX2 bus and is wrong here. */
+            const Uint64 _wd_now = SDL_GetTicksNS();
+            if (_wd_wait_start_ns == 0) {
+                _wd_wait_start_ns = _wd_now;
+            } else if ((_wd_now - _wd_wait_start_ns) >= DOS_AUDIO_WAITDEVICE_TIMEOUT_NS) {
+                doskutsu_dbg_log("audio: SDL/0063 WaitDevice ring-drain timeout "
+                                 "(%d ms) -- IRQ-5 wedged; returning to unblock "
+                                 "audio-thread teardown",
+                                 (int)(DOS_AUDIO_WAITDEVICE_TIMEOUT_NS / 1000000ULL));
+                /* patch 0055: P12 bracket exit (timeout path). */
+                if (g_p12_sdl_audio_instr_active) {
+                    g_p12_sdl_audio_thread_ns += SDL_GetTicksNS() - _p12_wait_t0;
+                }
+                return true;
+            }
+        }
+
         DOS_Yield();
     }
 }
@@ -847,6 +938,28 @@ static bool DOSSOUNDBLASTER_OpenDevice(SDL_AudioDevice *device)
         }
     }
 
+    /* DOSKUTSU patch (Phase 11 patch 0063): resolve the WaitDevice
+       ring-drain timeout killswitch once at OpenDevice time. Strict-"0"
+       match per CLAUDE.md env-var convention. Cached at module scope;
+       read by DOSSOUNDBLASTER_WaitDevice. Default ON: it guards the
+       quit-to-DOS teardown deadlock the 486-class campaign surfaced on
+       the Intel 486DX2 configs (docs/internal/486-CLASS-CAMPAIGN-
+       FINDINGS.md sec.9, Bug 2). */
+    {
+        const char *wdt_hint = SDL_GetHint("SDL_HINT_DOSKUTSU_DOS_AUDIO_WAITDEVICE_TIMEOUT");
+        g_dos_audio_waitdevice_timeout_disabled =
+            (wdt_hint && wdt_hint[0] == '0' && wdt_hint[1] == 0);
+        if (g_dos_audio_waitdevice_timeout_disabled) {
+            doskutsu_dbg_log("audio: SDL_HINT_DOSKUTSU_DOS_AUDIO_WAITDEVICE_TIMEOUT=0 "
+                             "(SDL/0063 ring-drain backstop DISABLED; WaitDevice "
+                             "reverts to pre-0063 unbounded spin)");
+        } else {
+            doskutsu_dbg_log("audio: SDL/0063 WaitDevice ring-drain timeout active "
+                             "(default; ~1000 ms backstop; quit-to-DOS teardown "
+                             "deadlock guard + WaitDevice-originated DSP resume)");
+        }
+    }
+
     /* DOSKUTSU patch (Phase 11 patch 0055): resolve P12 SDL-side audio
        instrumentation gates once at OpenDevice time. BOTH FLIP_INSTR
        AND P12_AUDIO_INSTR must be set to "1" (strict-"1" match) for
-- 
